<p>There's a reason this kitchen stops you mid-scroll. Designed by <strong>Chris Leach of Elevated Spaces</strong>, with interior design by <strong>Toni Mays Designs</strong>, it pairs two <a href="/brands/shiloh-cabinetry/">Shiloh Cabinetry</a> finishes to beautiful effect — and it's a clean lesson in how to use a two-finish kitchen to keep a big room from feeling flat.</p>

<div class="post-figure">
  <img src="/images/blog/elevated-spaces-shiloh-inset-kitchen/elevated-spaces-shiloh-showcase.webp" alt="Elevated Spaces Shiloh kitchen: white beaded inset maple perimeter in Polar, a rift cut white oak island and glass-front tall cabinet in Whitewash, brass hardware, dimensional white tile, and wood ceiling beams" width="1080" height="1350" fetchpriority="high" />
</div>

<h2>The two finishes</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Perimeter: beaded inset maple in Polar.</strong> Crisp and bright, with the bead detail giving the painted runs real craftsmanship up close.</li>
  <li><strong>Accent: rift cut white oak in Whitewash.</strong> Used on the oversized island and the glass-front tall cabinet, the warm, linear rift grain grounds the room.</li>
</ul>

<p>The mix of painted and natural wood keeps the room bright but layered — classic inset craftsmanship with a fresh, current feel.</p>

<h2>Why the combination works</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The wood goes where the eye lands.</strong> The island is the center of the room and the glass tall is the display piece, so that's where the warmth is. The perimeter stays quiet.</li>
  <li><strong>Rift grain reads modern next to traditional inset.</strong> Straight, linear rift oak keeps beaded inset from tipping into farmhouse.</li>
  <li><strong>Both finishes are top sellers.</strong> Maple Polar and Rift White Oak finishes are both on <a href="/playbook/top-10-finishes-species-2026/">W.W. Wood's Top 10 lists</a> — clients already like each one on its own, so the pairing is an easy yes.</li>
  <li><strong>Brass ties them together.</strong> Warm brass hardware and faucets bridge the white paint and the whitewashed oak.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Steal this for your next presentation</h2>

<p>When a client can't decide between a white kitchen and a wood kitchen, show them this one. Spec the perimeter in a painted inset and put one wood finish on the island and a single feature cabinet. It's also a strong showroom vignette: a painted inset run with a rift oak island lets you demonstrate two of the most-ordered looks in one display. Read more on <a href="/playbook/modern-inset/">Shiloh's inset options</a>.</p>

